{"author": "Ralph Waldo Emerson", "author_id": 1, "body": "Nothing can bring you peace but yourself. Nothing can bring you peace but the triumph of principles.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "b5e2d54d63f99e855fc4726322f2a6520288cfeb32c0cbd5c9c8a9d7b0b08dd1", "checked_ebook_id": 2944, "checked_end": 102428, "checked_start": 102328, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 78, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Self-Reliance", "source_location": "“Self-Reliance”, Essays: First Series (1841), last lines", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2944", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["calm", "self"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 1}
{"author": "Robert Louis Stevenson", "author_id": 23, "body": "Even if the doctor does not give you a year, even if he hesitates about a month, make one brave push and see what can be accomplished in a week.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "06d877a0afe5155d4fb8f9b9227c51851b0b13f5f87b0ae77e42ebcaf659291f", "checked_ebook_id": 386, "checked_end": 169786, "checked_start": 169642, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 79, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Æs Triplex", "source_location": "“Æs Triplex”, Virginibus Puerisque (1881)", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/386", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["courage", "time"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 85}
{"author": "Booker T. Washington", "author_id": 54, "body": "I would permit no man, no matter what his colour might be, to narrow and degrade my soul by making me hate him.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "a2886effe1d829ce9e3e23fade780c811d0d8f82a1a75594d33f2c6b937fe878", "checked_ebook_id": 2376, "checked_end": 223213, "checked_start": 223102, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 80, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Up from Slavery", "source_location": "Chapter VII, “Early Days at Tuskegee”", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2376", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["self", "courage"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 54}
{"author": "William Wordsworth", "author_id": 58, "body": "For oft when on my couch I lie\nIn vacant or in pensive mood,\nThey flash upon that inward eye\nWhich is the bliss of solitude,\nAnd then my heart with pleasure fills,\nAnd dances with the Daffodils.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "d15107c0717ea57b366497bf8c525e17d9effbb7e4ec83c6c75b9df8923c526e", "checked_ebook_id": 8824, "checked_end": 26415, "checked_start": 26211, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 81, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud", "source_location": "“I wandered lonely as a Cloud”, last stanza", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8824", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["nature", "calm"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 75}
{"author": "William Wordsworth", "author_id": 58, "body": "What though the radiance which was once so bright\nBe now for ever taken from my sight,\nThough nothing can bring back the hour\nOf splendour in the grass, of glory in the flower;\nWe will grieve not, rather find\nStrength in what remains behind.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "d15107c0717ea57b366497bf8c525e17d9effbb7e4ec83c6c75b9df8923c526e", "checked_ebook_id": 8824, "checked_end": 87495, "checked_start": 87212, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 82, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Ode: Intimations of Immortality", "source_location": "“Ode” (Intimations of Immortality), stanza 10", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8824", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["time", "calm"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 76}
{"author": "Marcus Aurelius", "author_id": 11, "body": "Look within. Within is the fountain of good, and it will ever bubble up, if thou wilt ever dig.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "c94855366014552f7d493b94db0c6484a36408179069fa54871d4c3a87f8dc75", "checked_ebook_id": 15877, "checked_end": 271208, "checked_start": 271113, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 83, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Meditations (trans. George Long)", "source_location": "Book VII, §59", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15877", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["self", "calm"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 12}
{"author": "Epictetus", "author_id": 20, "body": "Seek not that the things which happen should happen as you wish; but wish the things which happen to be as they are, and you will have a tranquil flow of life.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "e5d0bd27b8895f8691e9b15cce68c966c63f8c0813c94622f3c1ebc0df115837", "checked_ebook_id": 10661, "checked_end": 288796, "checked_start": 288637, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 84, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Enchiridion (trans. George Long)", "source_location": "Encheiridion VIII", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10661", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["calm"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 21}
{"author": "Confucius", "author_id": 22, "body": "Learning without thought is labour lost; thought without learning is perilous.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "120d414eafa89340d9329a37a10a87eecc997e7e40514ae4512113b76c25a17d", "checked_ebook_id": 3330, "checked_end": 9714, "checked_start": 9635, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 85, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Analects (trans. James Legge)", "source_location": "Book II (Wei Chang), ch. XV", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3330", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["self", "purpose"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 23}
{"author": "Charlotte Brontë", "author_id": 12, "body": "Life appears to me too short to be spent in nursing animosity or registering wrongs.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "1c2bd174cfa83d2f9603a8ce8c4186402eed3ea0ea39bfd534f9d67ccd0aa0fb", "checked_ebook_id": 1260, "checked_end": 123329, "checked_start": 123245, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 86, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "Jane Eyre", "source_location": "Chapter 6, Helen Burns", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1260", "speaker": "Helen Burns", "status": "live", "themes": ["calm", "love"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 13}
{"author": "Walt Whitman", "author_id": 6, "body": "That you are here—that life exists and identity,\nThat the powerful play goes on, and you may contribute a verse.", "body_format": [], "checked_at": "2026-10-29T01:59:19Z", "checked_body_sha256": "6e7277b238adac37570b0253341e84489c5fd47b20b82dcfabbdee6016ca472b", "checked_ebook_id": 1322, "checked_end": 381114, "checked_start": 380999, "checked_wikisource_oldid": null, "id": 87, "is_starter": false, "kind": "quote", "match_kind": "exact", "source": "O Me! O Life!", "source_location": "“O Me! O Life!” (Leaves of Grass, 1891–92), the answer", "source_url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1322", "speaker": null, "status": "live", "themes": ["purpose", "self"], "verifier_version": "5eac8affab7ea3d11dd1c66a4a3b48aaf08a65e5", "work_id": 73}
